      "text": "Given that the equation is equal to zero, we can start by setting $(x-5)^2=9$.\n\n$\\sqrt{(x-5)}=3$ or $\\sqrt{(x-5)}=-3$.\n\nNow we have two more variables $x$ and $y$ after we apply the square to get rid of the square root.\n\n$x-y=5$ or $x+y=5$. What I think is that $x$ and $y$ means that they are solutions for the given equation.\n\nNow we can solve for $y=5-x$ which represents $y$ for each $x$ and then arrange then all these $y$ values under one equation, $y=5-x$.\n\nUsing the two equations above $\\frac{5-3}{2}-\\frac{5+3}{2}$ = $\\boxed{0}$.\n\nQED",
